      deepTheory: `<p>The treatment room is a clinical workspace wearing a spa's clothing — every luxury cue must sit on top of genuine infection control. <b>Environmental parameters:</b> 24–26°C (client immobile under a blanket loses heat fast; cold clients tense facial muscles and skew your massage pressure), 40–60% relative humidity (dry air impairs barrier assessment — dehydrated readings on a parched room are false positives), dimmable warm-white light (~2700–3000K for ambience) plus a separate daylight-balanced task light (~5000–6500K magnifier lamp) because warm light hides erythema and pigment you must see. Ventilation must exchange air without blowing directly on the client (drafts chill damp skin and scatter powders/enzymes).</p><p><b>Cross-contamination architecture:</b> the one-way workflow — clean products and tools flow from closed storage to the trolley's clean zone; everything touching the client flows to the dirty zone then to disinfection or laundry, never backwards. Bowls are disinfected between clients (detergent clean → intermediate-level disinfectant at label contact time → air-dry); the bed vinyl is wiped between every client and linens changed fully (pillowcase, sheet, blanket cover, headband). Lotto of outbreaks in beauty settings trace to damp, warm reservoirs: steamer water (change per session, descale weekly), hot-towel cabinets (wash mitts per use, cabinet interior disinfected daily), and half-used jars (decant to single-use spatulas — fingers and used spatulas inoculate the whole jar with staphylococcus within days).</p><p><b>Ergonomics decides career length:</b> bed height set so your shoulders stay relaxed with elbows near 90° (adjustable electric beds pay for themselves in avoided back injury); stool with lumbar support and foot ring; magnifier lamp positioned so you look slightly down, not craning; trolley within arm's reach on your dominant side to eliminate twisting. Alternate standing/sitting across the day; six facials at a fixed low bed is how backs end.</p><p><b>Consent and boundaries, precisely:</b> the health form screens medications (isotretinoin, recent antibiotics, blood thinners), conditions (epilepsy, pregnancy, diabetes, cold-sore history), allergies (latex, nuts in oils, fragrance, aspirin/salicylates before BHA), and recent procedures (peels, laser, injectables — most need 2–4 week windows). Explain draping before it happens ('I'll step out; wrap the towel over your chest, lie face up'), announce every touch before contact ('I'm going to cleanse now — warm cloth coming'), and establish a stop signal for discomfort. Chaperone policy for intimate-adjacent work (full-body, under-18 with guardian present) must be written, not improvised.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — room reset checklist (between every client, under 7 minutes):</b> strip all linen to laundry bag → wipe bed vinyl + trolley + bowls (detergent, then disinfectant, full contact time) → fresh steamer water → restock spatulas, tissues, headband → lamp and device surfaces wiped → hands washed → next client's form and consent ready. Weekly: descale steamer, launder blankets, disinfect towel cabinet, check product expiry/PAO dates, test lamp bulbs and steamer output. Monthly: deep-clean vents and filters, audit retail shelf rotation, review incident log.</p>`,
