      deepTheory: `<p>Skin is a stratified, self-renewing barrier organ, and every facial decision flows from its architecture. <b>Epidermis (~0.1 mm, five sublayers):</b> basale (mitotic stem cells + melanocytes at ~1:10 ratio — the pigment factories), spinosum (desmosome-linked keratinocytes plus Langerhans immune sentinels), granulosum (keratohyalin granules begin cornification), lucidum (palms/soles only), corneum (15–20 layers of dead corneocytes in a lipid mortar of ceramides ~50%, cholesterol ~25%, free fatty acids ~15% — the bricks-and-mortar model). Transit time basale→shed averages ~28 days at 20, stretching to 40–60+ days with age — which is why mature skin needs assisted exfoliation and why retinoid 'purging' has a biological clock.</p><p><b>Dermis (1–4 mm):</b> papillary layer (loose collagen, capillary loops feeding the epidermis, Meissner touch receptors) and reticular layer (dense type-I/III collagen + elastin, pilosebaceous units, sweat glands, Pacinian pressure receptors). Fibroblasts manufacture collagen until UV (matrix metalloproteinases), sugar glycation and smoking degrade faster than synthesis — photoageing is enzymatically carved, not merely 'worn'. <b>Hypodermis</b> (adipose lobules + retaining ligaments) cushions and contours; its compartmental deflation with age drives the folds fillers target — your lane is supporting, not injecting.</p><p><b>Barrier physiology in numbers:</b> acid mantle pH 4.5–5.5 (enzymes that build and desquamate the corneum are pH-calibrated — alkaline cleansers stall desquamation and invite sensitivity); transepidermal water loss (TEWL) rises within minutes of barrier stripping (hot water, harsh foam, over-exfoliation) and normalises only with humectant + emollient + occlusive layering; natural moisturising factor (NMF — urea, lactates, amino acids) depletes with age and overwashing, which is why urea/lactic formulas rehabilitate winter skin. Melanin (eumelanin brown/black, pheomelanin red/yellow) caps keratinocyte nuclei against UV — Fitzpatrick I–VI predicts burn/tan behaviour and inflammatory hyperpigmentation risk, which must gate your exfoliant and heat choices on deeper tones.</p><p><b>Nutrition and systems links to advise (never diagnose):</b> protein + vitamin C + copper/zinc for collagen synthesis; essential fatty acids for the lipid mortar; vitamin A (not megadoses in pregnancy) for turnover; glycaemic load and dairy correlate with acne flares in susceptible clients — suggest a food-symptom diary, not a diet. Sleep deprivation raises cortisol and TEWL measurably; chronic stress impairs barrier recovery. Medications that change your protocol: isotretinoin (fragile, no wax/peel), topical retinoids (pause before strong exfoliation), photosensitisers (tetracyclines, thiazides — strict SPF counselling).</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — Fitzpatrick + exfoliation gating:</b> I–II (burns, freckles): aggressive peels tolerated with care, still patch-test; III–IV: moderate acids fine, watch post-inflammatory marks; V–VI: high PIH risk — prefer enzymes, low-% mandelic/lactic, strict SPF, no picking, heat kept low, test-patch behind the ear 48h for every new exfoliant. Barrier-distress signs that cancel exfoliation that day: stinging on bland moisturiser, visible tightness lines, flush that lingers &gt;20 min, recent sunburn. When in doubt, hydrate and rebook the peel.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The over-exfoliated regular', brief: 'A loyal client using daily glycolic pads, weekly scrubs and monthly strong peels presents with shiny, tight, sting-prone skin that flushes for hours. She asks for a stronger peel "since nothing is working". Diagnose and reset.', tasks: ['Name the condition (barrier impairment) and list the 4+ signs proving it versus true oiliness.', 'Explain the impaired-barrier science (lipid mortar, TEWL, pH enzymes) in client-friendly language.', 'Write the 4-week rehabilitation plan: what stops, what starts, in what order.', 'Script refusing the stronger peel while keeping her trust and bookings.'], debrief: 'Shine + sting + lingering flush = stripped barrier, not oiliness. Stop all acids/scrubs; bland ceramide cleanser, humectant + occlusive, SPF; reintroduce one gentle enzyme at week 4 only. Refusal script: "Your barrier is injured — a peel now would burn, not glow. Four weeks of repair, then results that last." Log and photograph the recovery.' },
