      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Analysis is differential diagnosis for the skin: separating the permanent from the changeable, then proving it with tests. <b>Types (genetic, stable over years):</b> normal/eudermic (balanced sebum + hydration, barely-visible pores, rare sensitivity); dry/alipidic (low sebum, fine pores, flaking, early fine lines — worsens with age and overwashing); oily/seborrhoeic (sebum excess from puberty/androgens, enlarged pores, congestion, slower wrinkling); combination (oily T-zone + normal/dry cheeks — the most common adult pattern, demanding zone-based care); sensitive (a reactivity modifier, not a standalone type — barrier-impaired or neurogenic, stings, flushes, reacts to fragrance/heat/alcohol).</p><p><b>Conditions (acquired, treatable):</b> dehydrated (water-deficient — crepey fine lines that vanish with hydration, tightness with or without oil, dullness; even oily skins dehydrate); sensitised (acquired reactivity from over-treatment, presents like sensitive but resolves with barrier repair); acne grades 1–4 (comedonal → papular → pustular/nodular → cystic/conglobate — grades 3–4 belong with a GP/dermatologist, squeezing them scars); pigmentation (melasma with dermal depth and hormonal drivers vs post-inflammatory marks vs solar lentigines — each with different prognosis); ageing phenotypes (intrinsic laxity vs photoageing texture/pigment — sunscreen history predicts which dominates).</p><p><b>The analysis sequence, parameterised:</b> (1) remove makeup/sunscreen fully, then observe bare skin for 10 minutes — immediate post-cleanse tightness misleads; (2) loupe scan zone by zone (forehead, nose/chin, cheeks, jaw, neck) under daylight-balanced light, noting pores, flakes, vessels, lesions, scars; (3) touch assessment — temperature (warm = active/inflamed), texture (rough = corneum build-up), turgor/elasticity bounce-back (slow = dehydration + age); (4) blot test — tissue pressed 10 seconds per zone, oil transfer graded 0–3; (5) Wood's lamp in darkness where available (coral-pink fluorescence = porphyrins/bacteria, white/blue-white = dehydration/thick corneum, brown-black = pigment depth, yellow-green = oily). Record T-zone and cheeks as separate skins — combination analysis done honestly often reveals two different facials in one face.</p><p><b>Documentation that holds up:</b> dated form with diagram of the face marked per zone (type + condition + severity 1–3), Fitzpatrick, contraindication screen, product history (actives with % where known), the agreed plan with review date, and photo consent + baseline photos in identical light. Re-analysis every 4–6 sessions or season change — skin migrates (summer-oily to winter-dry is normal), and static plans go stale. Multi-masking is the practical output: clay/T-zone, cream/cheeks, gel/sensitised areas — one face, three prescriptions.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — acne grade action table:</b> Grade 1 (open/closed comedones): salicylic/enzyme programme + extractions of open lesions, 4–6 weekly; Grade 2 (papules, few pustules): as above, gentler, no picking, consider GP if spreading; Grade 3 (multiple pustules/nodules): refer to GP/derm, supportive gentle facials only, zero extractions of inflamed lesions; Grade 4 (cystic/conglobate, scarring): immediate medical referral, camouflage and barrier support only. Scarring risk rises steeply from grade 2 — your extraction discipline at grade 1–2 prevents grade-4 faces.</p>`,
