      deepTheory: `<p>Cleansing, exfoliation and masking are controlled chemistry on living tissue — dose, pH and time decide whether you renew or injure. <b>Cleanser formulation literacy:</b> oil/balm first cleanses dissolve SPF, makeup and sebum ('like dissolves like') without stripping; second water-based cleansers finish the job — milky low-foam (dry/sensitive, pH ~5–5.5), gel (oily/combination, thorough but not squeaky — squeak means barrier lipids gone), enzyme-powder (gentle daily polish for tolerant skins). Double-cleanse at night always (SPF + day load); single morning cleanse suffices for dry skins (lukewarm water or milk). Massage 60 seconds — cleansing efficacy rises with contact time more than with harshness.</p><p><b>Exfoliant mechanisms, parameterised:</b> physical scrubs (jojoba beads, silica — round, non-jagged; never walnut/apricot shards that micro-tear; contraindicated on inflamed acne, rosacea flares, fragile capillaries); enzymes (papain, bromelain, pumpkin ferment — proteolytic, self-limiting, the sensitive-skin choice, 5–10 min); AHAs — glycolic (smallest molecule, deepest, most stimulating; 5–15% home, higher only professional with pH awareness), lactic (larger, hydrating, suits dry/sensitive and deeper tones), mandelic (largest, slowest, antibacterial — acne + tone-VI friendly); BHA salicylic (lipophilic — dives into pores, comedolytic + anti-inflammatory, 0.5–2% leave-on, 10–30% professional peels); PHA (gluconolactone — gentlest, humectant, post-procedure safe). Lower pH + higher % + longer time = deeper effect and higher risk; buffering, partial neutralisation and strict timing are the professional controls. Frequency ceiling 1–3×/week by strength; retinoid users and isotretinoin histories reduce it further.</p><p><b>Mask vehicles as delivery systems:</b> clay/kaolin-bentonite (cation exchange absorbs sebum and pulls softened comedones — T-zone, 10 min max, mist before cracking or it dehydrates by osmosis); cream (emollient-rich nourishment — dry/mature cheeks); gel/alginate (cooling hydration — sensitised, post-exfoliation); sheet (occlusion drives serum penetration — event glow, 15–20 min); modelling rubber masks (alginate seal over serum — luxury occlusive finish); charcoal variants (marketing over mechanism — judge by base formula, not colour). Never let clay crack on sensitive skin; never mask over undiagnosed rashes; eyes and lips get dedicated protection (damp pads, balm barrier).</p><p><b>Over-exfoliation recognition and rescue:</b> glossy 'plastic' shine, stinging on bland products, flush lingering over 20 minutes, breakouts in unacneic zones, products suddenly 'stopped working'. Rescue protocol: full acid/scrub/retinoid stop 2–4 weeks, bland ceramide cleanser, humectant + occlusive, mineral SPF, cool compresses; reintroduce one gentle enzyme only when bland moisturiser causes zero sensation for a week. Document the incident — over-exfoliation is the most common professional facial injury and entirely preventable by dose discipline.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — exfoliant selection matrix:</b> sensitive/redness-prone → enzyme 5–10 min; oily/congested → BHA 1–2% or salicylic programme; dull/fine lines, tolerant → AHA 8–15% glycolic/lactic; acne + deeper tones → mandelic or low-% salicylic; post-procedure/fragile → PHA only. Always: patch-test new exfoliants 48h on Fitzpatrick V–VI and reactive skins; one exfoliant per routine (no scrub + acid stacking); SPF non-negotiable after every exfoliating facial — acids are photosensitisers by mechanism.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The shiny-stingy regular', brief: 'A client on daily scrub + nightly 10% glycolic + weekly clay full-face presents glossy, sting-prone, breaking out on cheeks where she never broke out. She wants a stronger peel. Intervene.', tasks: ['Diagnose with 4+ signs distinguishing over-exfoliation from true oiliness.', 'Explain the mechanism (lipid loss, TEWL, pH enzyme stall) in two client-friendly sentences.', 'Write the 3-week rescue with stop/start sequencing and review markers.', 'Refuse the stronger peel in writing-tone language that retains her.'], debrief: 'Unacneic-zone breakouts + bland-product sting + lingering flush = over-exfoliated. Full stop 2–4 weeks, bland repair, SPF; reintroduce single enzyme at week 4. Refusal frames repair as the faster route to glow — with photos proving it at review.' },
