      deepTheory: `<p>The 60-minute European facial is choreography: ten stations sequenced so each prepares the next, timed so the hour holds six appointments a day without rushing. <b>Station timing budget:</b> consult + drape 5 → first cleanse 3 → analysis 5 → second cleanse + exfoliate 7 → steam 5–7 → extractions 5 (only if indicated) → massage 10 → mask 12 (with hand/arm massage during setting) → tone/serum/moisturise 5 → SPF + home-care plan 3. Total 60–62. Run over and the column collapses; run under by skipping and results collapse — pacing is the skill.</p><p><b>Steam physics and limits:</b> warm vapour (40–45°C at skin distance, nozzle ~30 cm) hydrates the corneum, softens comedonal plugs and boosts subsequent product penetration — it does NOT 'open pores' (pores have no muscle). Limits: rosacea/couperose (heat vasodilates visibly), uncontrolled asthma (humidity load), claustrophobia, recent injectables/peels per window, pregnancy comfort. Towel-steam alternative for contraindicated clients. Ozone steamers (sparking 'fresh' smell) add mild antibacterial effect but irritate sensitive eyes and asthmatics — know your unit.</p><p><b>Extraction discipline (the litigation hotspot):</b> candidates are open comedones and fully surfaced pustules only — never closed comedones (rupture inward → nodules), never papules/nodules/cysts, never the nasal triangle depths beyond skill, never more than ~5 minutes of attempts per face. Technique: tissue-wrapped fingers or sterile loop, gentle lateral pressure after steam + desincrustation, stop at blood/pain/blanching that doesn't resolve. Post-extraction: antiseptic/cooling (high-frequency or cool compress where trained), calming mask, strict no-pick home instruction. Document count and areas; photograph congestion baselines so courses show progress.</p><p><b>Layering pharmacology of the finish:</b> toner restores pH and damp-preps for humectants; serum (small molecules, high actives — vitamin C, niacinamide, peptides) goes on damp skin; moisturiser seals by type (gel/lotion/cream per zone); SPF 30+ broad-spectrum finishes every daytime facial (two finger-lengths, reapplication counselled). Eye and lip zones get dedicated products — facial acids and fragranced creams migrate and irritate. The home-care close prescribes max three items with doses and order written on the aftercare card; courses (4–6 weekly) are offered before the client sits up, priced per session and total.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — extraction decision tree:</b> open comedone + softened + superficial → extract gently, one attempt; closed comedone → leave, treat with salicylic programme over sessions; papule/nodule/cyst → never touch, medical referral pathway; pustule with visible head → single gentle attempt or leave; resistance, pain or blood → stop immediately, cool, note. Maximum 5 minutes per face — a facial is not an extraction appointment; sell the course, not the squeeze.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The extraction bruise', brief: 'A client returns with post-facial bruising and broken capillaries across both cheeks after an enthusiastic 20-minute extraction session by a junior (you, last week). She is upset. Handle the recovery and the learning.', tasks: ['Write the immediate response: apology framing, soothing protocol, review booking.', 'Identify every protocol breach (candidate selection, time limit, pressure, aftercare).', 'Set the healing plan: what she uses/avoids for 7 days, when you review.', 'Write the personal rule set preventing recurrence (timer, candidate list, stop signals).'], debrief: 'Own it, soothe with cool + barrier, review at 48h and 7 days, no charge for correction. Breaches: wrong candidates, 4× time limit, pressure past pain, likely no calming close. New rules: 5-minute timer visible, open-comedones-only list on the trolley, blood/pain = instant stop. Log as an incident, not a secret.' },
