      deepTheory: `<p>Triage separates treatable from referable in under five minutes — and the wrong call injures people or your insurance. <b>Acne vulgaris, graded precisely:</b> grade 1 comedonal (open blackheads, closed whiteheads — programme + open extractions); grade 2 papular (red papules, few pustules — gentle programme, no picking, GP threshold if spreading); grade 3 pustular/nodular (multiple pustules, painful nodules — medical referral, supportive facials only, zero inflamed extractions); grade 4 cystic/conglobate (deep cysts, sinus tracts, scarring — immediate dermatology, camouflage + barrier support only). Aggravators to screen: picking, comedogenic oils/pomades, anabolic supplements, high-glycaemic load, friction (masks, helmets, phones), picking-adjacent stress. Isotretinoin, hormonal therapy and antibiotics are medical lanes — you support, never second-guess.</p><p><b>Rosacea (four subtypes, often coexisting):</b> erythematotelangiectatic (flushing, persistent redness, visible vessels — no heat/steam/alcohol-based products, cool techniques, mineral SPF, trigger diary: heat, alcohol, spicy food, UV); papulopustular (acne-like but no comedones — gentle, no picking, GP for prescription options); phymatous (tissue thickening, usually nose — dermatology); ocular (gritty eyes, lid inflammation — ophthalmology/GP, no eye-area actives). Steroid-induced rosacea-like dermatitis from misused topical steroids needs medical withdrawal supervision — recognise the rebound pattern and refer.</p><p><b>Dermatitis/eczema patterns:</b> atopic (flexural, dry, itchy, barrier-deficient — bland repair only, flare = postpone facial), contact irritant (sharp-bordered, matches exposure — remove cause, cool, refer if spreading) vs allergic (delayed 24–72h, spreading beyond contact — patch-test detective work, GP/dermatology), seborrhoeic (oily zones, yellowish scale — gentle antifungal-adjunct routines per GP advice). Herpes simplex (tingling prodrome → clustered vesicles on erythema — no treatment over active lesions, strict no-touch cross-infection protocol, antivirals are GP-prescribed); impetigo (honey-coloured crusts, highly contagious — refuse, refer); warts/molluscum (no treatment over, refer for removal pathways); suspicious pigmented lesions (ABCDE: asymmetry, border, colour, diameter >6mm, evolution — any evolving lesion gets a 2-week GP referral recommendation, never treated over, photographed only with consent).</p><p><b>Reaction drill, parameterised:</b> mild irritation (remove product fully with cool water, cool compress 10 min, bland barrier, observe 30 min, document, evening check-in); spreading urticaria/swelling (remove, cool, advise same-day GP/pharmacist, document with photos, follow up 24h); anaphylaxis signs (lip/tongue swelling, breathing difficulty, dizziness — emergency services immediately, keep client seated upright, note time of onset and product for paramedics). Every room holds: incident forms, emergency numbers posted, antihistamine-free policy (never administer drugs), and a stocked first-aid kit. Log everything — near-misses included — and review monthly for patterns.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — RAG triage card (laminate for the trolley):</b> RED refuse + refer: fever/acute infection, impetigo, active herpes in treatment zone, undiagnosed lumps, severe eczema flare, suspected tinea, evolving moles. AMBER adapt + consent: mild rosacea (no heat), controlled acne 1–2 (no inflamed extraction), diabetes (slow healing, gentle pressure), pregnancy (no retinoids/strong acids, positioning), well-controlled eczema (avoid plaques). GREEN proceed: intact healthy skin with managed chronic conditions noted. When torn between amber and red, choose red — caution never injured anyone.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The wedding-week breakout', brief: 'A bride, 5 days out, presents with grade-3 pustular acne across the cheeks that she has been squeezing. She begs for extractions and a strong peel "to clear it before Saturday". Triage under pressure.', tasks: ['Grade and triage with justification (why grade 3, why red/amber, what is forbidden).', 'Write the safe 5-day plan: what you do in-room, what she does at home, what you refuse.', 'Script the refusal of extractions/peel that keeps her calm and booked.', 'Define the GP-referral wording and the camouflage/make-up-day contingency.'], debrief: 'Grade 3 + picking + deadline = no extractions, no strong peel (scarring + rebound risk before the wedding). Plan: calming anti-inflammatory facial, LED if available, hands-off home routine, GP fast-track note, professional camouflage trial for Saturday. Honesty now prevents scars in the photos forever.' },
