      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Product chemistry is INCI literacy plus dose discipline — marketing claims are unregulated poetry, ingredient lists are facts. <b>Reading INCI:</b> descending order to 1%, then any order (so hero botanicals listed last may sit at 0.01%); 'dermatologically tested' means tested, not suitable; 'hypoallergenic' and 'natural' have no legal definition in most markets; fragrance/parfum (including essential oils like lavender, citrus, tea tree) is the top cosmetic allergen group — sensitive and barrier-impaired skins avoid it; PAO symbol (open jar, e.g. 12M) and expiry dates are safety data, check them on back-bar stock quarterly.</p><p><b>Active profiles with parameters:</b> niacinamide 2–5% (pores, tone, barrier — the beginner-friendly workhorse; 10%+ flushes sensitive skins); vitamin C — L-ascorbic 10–20% at pH <3.5 (brightening, collagen cofactor; unstable, tingling normal, AM + SPF mandatory) vs MAP/SAP derivatives (gentler, slower); retinoids — retinol 0.25–1% PM, retinal faster-converting, tretinoin prescription-only; pea-size, 2–3 nights/week ramping over 6–8 weeks, sandwich method (moisturiser sandwich) for beginners, SPF non-negotiable, contraindicated in pregnancy (all retinoids); AHAs 5–15% home (see Week 4 matrix); BHA 0.5–2% leave-on; azelaic 10–20% (rosacea-safe brightening, pregnancy-compatible option); peptides (signal carriers — supportive, slow, best in leave-ons); ceramides/cholesterol/fatty acids in ~2:1:1-ish ratio thinking for barrier repair; hyaluronic multi-weight (surface + deeper hydration — always on damp skin, always sealed).</p><p><b>Vehicle matching (the delivery half of efficacy):</b> gels/serums (oily, humid climates, layered routines); lotions (combination, day); creams (dry, night, barrier repair); oils/balms (very dry, occlusive finish, PM only); silicone-heavy primers (texture smoothing — warn acne-prone clients about comedogenic load). Order rule thin→thick, water→oil, treat→seal; wait ~60 seconds between layers for penetration; SPF 30+ broad-spectrum last every morning (two finger-lengths face + neck, reapply every 2 hours outdoors).</p><p><b>Prescription method:</b> diagnose → max three products (cleanser-adjust, one active, moisturiser/SPF anchor) → dose in writing (pea, pump, finger-lengths) → introduction order with 2-week gaps → review date with photo baseline. One new active at a time isolates tolerance; starting three actives together makes reactions untraceable. Home-care non-compliance causes most 'treatment failures' — follow-up messages at 48h and 2 weeks ('any stinging? send a photo') convert compliance and rebookings simultaneously.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — retinoid ramp card (give to every starter):</b> weeks 1–2: pea-size, 2 nights/week, sandwich method, SPF daily; weeks 3–4: 3 nights if zero irritation; weeks 5–8: alternate nights; maintenance: nightly as tolerated. Pause rules: stinging on bland moisturiser, flaking that makeup cannot cover, sunburn, waxing/exfoliating facial within 5–7 days either side. Pregnancy/planning: stop all retinoids, switch to azelaic + niacinamide + SPF pathway with obstetric awareness.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The three-serum pile-up', brief: 'A client started vitamin C 20%, retinol 1% nightly AND daily glycolic toner in the same week (all full-face). She now has burning, peeling and new breakouts, and blames "the facial you gave me". Untangle it.', tasks: ['Explain the overload mechanism (barrier + penetration stacking) and why blame attribution matters commercially.', 'Write the strip-back routine: exact products, order, doses, and what stops today.', 'Set the reintroduction sequence with gaps and tolerance checkpoints.', 'Draft the message that resets the relationship and books the review.'], debrief: 'Three new actives = untraceable chaos on a stripped barrier. Strip to bland cleanse + moisturise + SPF; reintroduce one active per 2 weeks starting gentlest (niacinamide), C before retinoid, acids last. Message owns the guidance gap ("I should have sequenced these for you"), books a free 2-week review — accountability converts anger to loyalty.' },
