      deepTheory: `<p>Energy devices multiply facial results — and multiply liability. Foundation rule: understand mechanisms, respect contraindications absolutely, log every parameter, and never exceed your training scope (lasers, deep peels, injectables and microneedling depth are advanced/medical lanes, not this course). <b>High-frequency (Tesla current via glass electrodes):</b> argon (violet, acne/oily) and neon (orange-red, ageing/dry) gases fluoresce; the rapidly alternating current creates germicidal ozone + mild thermal stimulation + increased perfusion. Technique: lowest effective intensity, electrode in contact or 5 mm spark-gap, constant motion, 3–5 minutes total, dry-ish skin with light gauze barrier (never over wet gels that conduct unpredictably, never over broken skin). Absolute contraindications: pacemakers/metal implants in the current path, epilepsy, pregnancy, rosacea flare, broken/inflamed skin, recent injectables per window. Remove metal jewellery, no watches on your hands, client removes theirs.</p><p><b>LED photobiomodulation, dosed properly:</b> red ~630–660 nm (fibroblast stimulation, collagen support, calming — 10–20 min, 4–20 J/cm² typical panel doses), near-infrared ~830–850 nm (deeper, often combined with red), blue ~415 nm (porphyrin excitation in C. acnes — antibacterial, 8–15 min), with green/amber as adjunctive tone claims. Parameters that matter: wavelength accuracy, irradiance (mW/cm²), distance (dose falls with the square of distance — panels have fixed protocols for a reason), eye protection (goggles for client AND operator with high-output panels), clean dry skin, photosensitising drugs screened (tetracyclines, isotretinoin, St John's wort). LED supports courses; it does not replace extraction, exfoliation or home-care.</p><p><b>Microcurrent (low-level, subsensory current mimicking endogenous bioelectricity):</b> upward gliding strokes with conductive gel, 5–20 minutes per zone programme (NuFace/Ziip-style protocols), immediate lifting/sculpting via muscle re-education claims plus ATP/collagen-support marketing — sell visible short-term toning plus course-based improvement, never permanent restructuring. Contraindications mirror HF plus: active cancer, thrombosis/phlebitis zones, open epilepsy, pregnancy (manufacturer-dependent — default to avoid). <b>Ultrasonic scrubbers</b> (24–28 kHz spatula cavitation lifts surface debris and softened comedones — gentle blackhead maintenance, not deep extraction replacement; keep the blade flat, skin taut and wet); <b>galvanic desincrustation/iontophoresis</b> (direct current softens sebum then drives ionised serums — know polarity, never over rosacea/broken skin); <b>oxygen infusion</b> (pressurised O₂ + serum mist — soothing luxury finish with modest evidence, excellent sensitive-skin theatre).</p><p><b>Device governance:</b> patch/intensity test every new client; written consent naming the device + expected sensation + session count; parameter log (device, applicator, intensity/fluence, time, skin response, adverse events); maintenance (electrode integrity, panel output checks, hygiene sleeves/barriers); adverse-event pathway (burn/blister → cool, cover, document, GP/derm referral, manufacturer report). No-go stack for every device facial: undisclosed pregnancy, implanted electronics, epilepsy, active infection/herpes in zone, accutane window, suspicious lesions. When a device alarms, errors or behaves unexpectedly — stop, do not improvise.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — device parameter card (fill per session):</b> device + applicator | intensity/fluence setting | wavelength/mode | zone-by-zone time | total time | skin response (1–5 + notes) | adverse events (none/describe) | next-session adjustment. Review logs monthly: consistent non-responders need plan changes, not more energy. Client take-home: expected sensations timeline (mild warmth/tingling normal; pain, blistering, spreading redness = call us today).</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The undisclosed pacemaker', brief: 'Midway through a high-frequency add-on you notice a pacemaker scar plus a medical alert bracelet the consultation missed. The client says "it\'s fine, keep going". Decide and act.', tasks: ['State the stop decision with the mechanism (current + implanted electronics).', 'Script halting the service without alarming her, including what you do with the scheduled time.', 'Rebuild the consultation screen that missed this (questions, form design, bracelet check).', 'Design the safe alternative facial you offer instead, with pricing handled fairly.'], debrief: 'Stop immediately — client permission cannot override absolute contraindications. Convert remaining time to a luxury manual facial at adjusted price, document the near-miss as an incident. New screen: explicit implanted-device + epilepsy + pregnancy questions read aloud, bracelet/jewellery visual check before current touches skin. Near-misses get logged like injuries.' },
