      deepTheory: `<p>The business of skin is retention mathematics plus trust infrastructure. <b>Column economics:</b> new-client acquisition costs 5–7× retaining one (advertising, offers, empty gaps); a course-based column with >60% pre-booking and >70% maintenance conversion is stable almost regardless of marketing. Pricing psychology: anchor with three tiers (essential/course/luxury — most clients choose the middle), price courses per-session AND total (transparency converts), never discount results (discount the add-on or the retail, not the expertise), and raise prices with documented demand (waitlist >2 weeks = 10–15% rise signal).</p><p><b>Consultation-to-course conversion script:</b> diagnose aloud with the mirror ('see how the T-zone shines while the cheeks flake?'), name the goal in the client's words, show the staged plan with session count, state total + per-session + home-care investment plainly, offer two start times, handle the pause ('shall I hold Thursday while you check your diary?'), and follow up once with value in 48h. Track conversion rate (consultations → courses); below 30% usually means diagnosis isn't voiced or pricing isn't itemised, not that clients 'can't afford it'.</p><p><b>Reviews and portfolio law:</b> ask at the glow moment with a QR code (timing beats scripting); respond to every review within 48h (thanks + specifics for praise, acknowledge + invite-back for criticism — never argue publicly); before/after photos need explicit written consent stating channels (portfolio/social/ads) plus identical light/angle; never use filters, stock, or other practitioners' work; incentivised reviews must be declared per advertising rules. One detailed honest review with photos outperforms fifty stars.</p><p><b>Complaint and incident governance:</b> written recovery policy (review windows: 7 days treatments, 14 days courses), incident forms with photos, monthly pattern review (same fault twice = system fix: retraining, protocol change, product swap), insurance notification thresholds understood, patch-test and consent archives retrievable. <b>Assessment standard:</b> 30-question theory ≥70% plus observed full European facial with massage, home-care pitch and course plan against the rubric — distinction at 85%+. The certificate means identical things for every graduate, which is what lets employers trust it.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — monthly business dashboard (1st of month, 30 min):</b> pre-book rate (target &gt;60%) | course conversion (target &gt;30%) | maintenance conversion (target &gt;70%) | retail attach (target 20–30%) | complaint rate (target &lt;2%, all logged) | review velocity (target ≥4 new/month) | waitlist length (pricing signal). One page, five minutes to update if cards are kept properly — the numbers tell you whether to market, retrain, reprice or rest.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The quiet column', brief: 'Three months post-qualification: skilled facials, 5-star reviews from friends, but a half-empty column, 15% pre-booking, near-zero retail. Diagnose the business, not the technique.', tasks: ['Rank the leaks: no course offers? no pre-book script? silent diagnosis? no follow-up? no review engine?', 'Write the exact pre-book and course-offer scripts she will use from Monday.', 'Design her 30-day turnaround: review engine, reactivation messages, retail attach method.', 'Set the dashboard targets and the date she reviews them with her mentor.'], debrief: 'Technique is rarely the leak — conversion systems are. Scripts: diagnose aloud + two-times offer + 48h value follow-up. 30 days: QR at glow, reactivate lapsed clients once with value, attach retail by demonstration, track all five metrics. Review with mentor on day 30 — accountability closes the loop.' },
